{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UB2AsAYf4smC2iDNRTJ4tm","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall Score: 5/10 (Marginally Below Threshold)**\n\n**Core strengths:** Unprecedented scale (5,227 models x 12,508 items), well-designed control conditions (random split at 0.18% is a strong null baseline), the cross-subject matching protocol is a genuine methodological contribution (Delta-FPR = 0.000), and the ground truth meta-critique (global vs. differential contamination) is conceptually valuable with implications beyond this paper.\n\n**Critical weaknesses identified:**\n\n1. **Temporal confound unresolved.** The 31% instability cannot be separated from legitimate capability evolution. The within-family comparison (Llama-2 vs. Llama-3 at 61.3%) actually suggests genuine progress produces *more* DIF, undermining the \"instability\" interpretation.\n\n2. **Misleading framing.** DIF in educational testing signals item bias; in the LLM setting it may simply reflect that models changed non-uniformly across items -- which is expected and desirable. The alarming 31% headline is undercut by rho=0.997 ranking preservation.\n\n3. **Low ecological validity of injection framework.** Binary response flipping does not model real contamination mechanisms (partial memorization, format priming, selective retrieval).\n\n4. **Catastrophic IRT misfit** (98% of models with |Lz| > 2, CFI=0.41) undermines the psychometric conceptual apparatus the paper depends on.\n\n5. **Manuscript appears incomplete.** SIBTEST analysis, alternative method comparisons, negative results, and practitioner's guide referenced in the summary are absent from the paper text."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a722f1afaa51a1652 (use SendMessage with to: 'a722f1afaa51a1652' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 46984\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 188697</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b56c3912-c718-44fa-8fad-b4f0c1958f5c","uuid":"91058561-36dc-4781-b5af-db817ab521ee","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:34:59.245Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an ICLR submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Produce a detailed, rigorous review.\n\n## Venue & Scoring\nICLR, 1-10 scale: 10=Strong Accept, 8=Accept, 6=Marginally Above threshold, 5=Marginally Below threshold, 3=Reject, 1=Strong Reject.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Empirical Characterization of Measurement Stability in LLM Benchmarks: Challenges and an Open Framework\"\n\nThe paper applies Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis from psychometrics to METABENCH MMLU (5,227 models × 12,508 items). Key contributions:\n1. Applies both Mantel-Haenszel (MH) and SIBTEST to find ≥18-31% of MMLU items exhibit functionally unstable measurement properties across model generations.\n2. Proposes a semi-synthetic validation framework (ΔTPR methodology) with cross-subject matching that eliminates FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000).\n3. Shows instability doesn't threaten ranking validity (ρ≈0.997 between full and stable-only rankings).\n4. Ground-truth meta-critique: web-overlap/n-gram labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination — they can't validate item-level differential detectors.\n5. Compares DIF against ConStat perturbation testing, DVD generation variance, Item-Focused Trees, and multi-signal ensemble fusion.\n6. Reports extensive negative results: bifactor IRT equating fails (CFI=0.72, TLI=0.69), LLM item generation fails all metrics, RL robustness underpowered (N=13), Bayesian cumulative DIF insufficient gain, ensemble doesn't beat best single signal.\n7. Provides a practitioner's guide.\n\n## Detailed Paper Content\n\n**Section 3 (Method):**\n- §3.1 MH-DIF: Stratifies models by total score, computes α_MH and Δ_MH = -2.35 ln(α_MH). ETS classification: A (negligible, |Δ|<1.0), B (moderate, 1.0≤|Δ|<1.5), C (large, |Δ|≥1.5). MH detects only uniform DIF.\n- §3.2 SIBTEST: Partitions items into studied/matching subtests, detects both uniform and non-uniform (crossing) DIF. Threshold: β̂ ≥ 0.088.\n- §3.3 Semi-Synthetic Validation: Injects differential contamination (flip incorrect→correct with exploitation rate p∈[0,1]) into real METABENCH response matrix. Measures ΔTPR(p)=TPR(p)-TPR(0). Cross-subject matching: conditions MH on total score across other subjects to eliminate matching-variable contamination. ΔFPR measures false-positive inflation.\n- §3.4 Alternative Methods: ConStat (237 reformulated items, semantic similarity=0.91), DVD (500 items, 12 models, 50 responses/item, temperature 0.7), Item-Focused Trees (release date as continuous covariate).\n\n**Section 4 (Experiments):**\n- §4.1 Temporal DIF on MMLU: MH flags 31.3% as ETS C. SIBTEST flags 44.7%. Jaccard overlap only 0.48. Random-split sanity: C%=0.18%. Ability-stratified: C%=2.9%. Within-family (Llama-2 vs Llama-3): 61.3%. Architecture stratification: 99.98% decoder-only, removing 1 non-decoder model changes nothing. IRT fit remains poor (CFI=0.41, RMSEA=0.089, 97.8-98.1% models |Lz|>2).\n- §4.2 Ground-Truth Meta-Critique: DIF enrichment against Li et al. clean labels ≈0.96. This is expected because web-overlap measures global, not differential exposure.\n- §4.3 Semi-Synthetic Validation: ΔTPR increases monotonically from 0.222 (p=0.3) to 0.674 (p=1.0). ΔFPR=0.000 across all conditions. Within-subject matching inflates FPR by +0.186. Paper acknowledges binary flip is simplification (Table 5: signal type, response pattern, item correlation, effect gradient all HIGH/MEDIUM mismatch with real contamination).\n- §4.4 GSM8K Replication: C%=18.9% (standard temporal), 47.4% (external), 0.3% (sanity). ΔTPR=+0.818 (standard). DIF-C items systematically harder (mean difficulty 0.38 vs 0.61, p<0.001) — difficulty confound acknowledged.\n- §4.5 Downstream Impact: ρ≈0.997 (ranking redundancy). Circularity ablation: DIF removal z=-9.5 (least disruptive non-random strategy). DIF-discrimination overlap Jaccard=0.12. Graceful degradation: critical threshold ∼23%, but DIF flag rate is 31% — tension acknowledged.\n- §4.6 Alternative Methods: ConStat AUROC 0.78 (non-RL) vs DIF 0.73, FPR 0.067 vs 0.314 — ConStat better on non-RL. DVD AUROC 0.69 (verbatim), 0.58 (variant), bimodal only 23%. IFT: FPR 0.088, TPR 0.59 vs MH 0.68, overfitting CV gap 0.12, 8/12 splits unstable. Ensemble: weighted AUROC 0.76 < ConStat alone 0.78, DeLong p=0.38, CV fold variance 0.034, 3/8 configs exceed FPR 0.10.\n- §5.1 Negative Results: RL robustness (N=13, AUROC 0.82→0.64, bootstrap CI [0.41,0.84], TOST p=0.31). Bifactor IRT (CFI=0.72, TLI=0.69, failed convergence, score equivalence fails at all levels). LLM item generation (all 6 metrics FAIL). Bayesian cumulative DIF (power 0.63 at 4 admins vs 0.70 target, prior miscalibration compounds).\n- §5.2 Key Limitations: IRT misspecification (98% |Lz|>2), no ground truth, temporal confounding, scalability of alternatives.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference\nThe paper covers all 13 planned experiments from the design document. No planned experiment is missing from the paper.\n\n## Your Task\nProduce a thorough review focused on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Are the methods (MH, SIBTEST, ΔTPR framework, cross-subject matching) correctly applied? Are statistical tests appropriate?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are experiments well-designed? Are controls adequate? Are conclusions supported by the data?\n3. **Internal consistency**: Do the results across experiments tell a coherent story?\n4. **Weaknesses in methods/experiments**: What are the critical methodological gaps?\n\nYour review must include:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted list)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted list, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for Authors (numbered list)\n- Scores: Soundness (1-4), Presentation (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Overall (1-10), Confidence (1-5)\n- One paragraph justification of overall score\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Do not mention any tier designation. Evaluate the paper on its own merits.\n","agentId":"a722f1afaa51a1652","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall Score: 5/10 (Marginally Below Threshold)**\n\n**Core strengths:** Unprecedented scale (5,227 models x 12,508 items), well-designed control conditions (random split at 0.18% is a strong null baseline), the cross-subject matching protocol is a genuine methodological contribution (Delta-FPR = 0.000), and the ground truth meta-critique (global vs. differential contamination) is conceptually valuable with implications beyond this paper.\n\n**Critical weaknesses identified:**\n\n1. **Temporal confound unresolved.** The 31% instability cannot be separated from legitimate capability evolution. The within-family comparison (Llama-2 vs. Llama-3 at 61.3%) actually suggests genuine progress produces *more* DIF, undermining the \"instability\" interpretation.\n\n2. **Misleading framing.** DIF in educational testing signals item bias; in the LLM setting it may simply reflect that models changed non-uniformly across items -- which is expected and desirable. The alarming 31% headline is undercut by rho=0.997 ranking preservation.\n\n3. **Low ecological validity of injection framework.** Binary response flipping does not model real contamination mechanisms (partial memorization, format priming, selective retrieval).\n\n4. **Catastrophic IRT misfit** (98% of models with |Lz| > 2, CFI=0.41) undermines the psychometric conceptual apparatus the paper depends on.\n\n5. **Manuscript appears incomplete.** SIBTEST analysis, alternative method comparisons, negative results, and practitioner's guide referenced in the summary are absent from the paper text."}],"totalDurationMs":188697,"totalTokens":46984,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6829,"cache_read_input_tokens":39759,"output_tokens":395,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6829},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":395,"cache_read_input_tokens":39759,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6829,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6829,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":10,"searchCount":7,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":2}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"aea3ad15f794d3072","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JmYSHry4Z4A85r6Rtdg3Cs","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":86264,"tool_uses":27,"duration_ms":183830},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"ad8d023c-26f3-41db-9f89-5b5aab5a1903","session_id":"b56c3912-c718-44fa-8fad-b4f0c1958f5c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01R9SoHNyK9w8RaxpLd9oUmV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TbpNPCyq6ez1PBKuVX9Fhj","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty/Significance and Related Work/Positioning\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper applies Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis from educational psychometrics to the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models x 12,508 items), finding that 31% of items exhibit large DIF between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts. The paper also proposes a semi-synthetic validation framework (ΔTPR methodology with cross-subject matching), critiques the use of web-overlap labels as ground truth for differential detectors, and replicates on GSM8K. While the scale of analysis is impressive and the transparency about negative results is commendable, the paper applies an unmodified classical method to a new domain and the resulting insights, though competently executed, are incremental rather than surprising.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Honest reporting of negative results.** The paper is admirably transparent about what did not work (bifactor IRT, item generation, Bayesian DIF, ensemble fusion, RL robustness). This is rare and valuable for the community.\n\n- **Ground truth meta-critique is well-argued.** The distinction between global exposure and differential functioning (Section 4.3/5.2) is a genuine conceptual contribution. The observation that web-overlap labels cannot serve as ground truth for differential detectors is correct and has not been stated this clearly before. This should influence how future contamination detection papers validate their methods.\n\n- **Cross-subject matching is a clean methodological insight.** The ΔFPR = 0.000 result under cross-subject matching, and the clear demonstration of why within-subject matching inflates FPR (because the conditioning variable absorbs the injection signal), is a practical and reusable insight that survives scrutiny independently of the other claims.\n\n- **Scale.** The analysis on 5,227 x 12,508 is the largest DIF analysis applied to LLM benchmarks, and the random-split sanity check (0.18% C%) is a convincing null control.\n\n- **Well-structured paper with clear claims.** The four numbered claims in the introduction are explicitly stated and systematically addressed. The mapping between claims and evidence is easy to follow.\n\n- **GSM8K replication.** Extending to a second benchmark with an independent contamination validation mechanism (GSM1K) strengthens the generalizability argument.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n1. **Limited methodological novelty (Major).** The paper explicitly states \"we do not modify the MH-DIF procedure itself; the contributions are empirical, not algorithmic.\" MH-DIF is a 1988 method with extensive textbook treatment. Applying an off-the-shelf statistical test to a new dataset, even at unprecedented scale, constitutes an empirical contribution, not a methodological one. The cross-subject matching protocol is sensible but straightforward — it is the standard recommendation when the matching variable may be contaminated (Holland & Thayer 1988 already discuss matching variable selection). The ΔTPR/ΔFPR reporting framework is simply measuring incremental detection rates under injection, which is standard in detection evaluation. For ICLR, I expect either (a) novel methods adapted to the LLM setting, or (b) empirical findings that fundamentally change how the community operates. This paper is closer to (b) but does not quite reach the bar (see weakness #3).\n\n2. **The 31% headline number is method-dependent and potentially inflated (Major).** The paper reports SIBTEST flags 44.7% of items with only 0.48 Jaccard overlap against MH. This means MH and SIBTEST disagree on which items are unstable more often than they agree. The paper acknowledges the instability range is \"≥18-31% depending on DIF type and threshold,\" but the title and abstract still anchor on \"31%.\" The discrepancy between MH and SIBTEST indicates substantial non-uniform (crossing) DIF that MH misses by design — items where the DIF direction reverses across the ability spectrum. If the instrument's core finding is this sensitive to methodological choice, the paper should either (a) present a principled way to reconcile the methods, or (b) reframe the headline entirely around the method-dependence as the finding. The current framing leans heavily on 31% while burying the SIBTEST comparison.\n\n3. **Instability is not actionable (Major).** The paper finds 31% of items are unstable, then shows this does not affect rankings (ρ ≈ 0.997). The DIF analysis identifies *that* items changed but not *why*. The paper cannot distinguish contamination from legitimate capability gains from training methodology changes from data composition shifts. Without causal attribution, the finding is descriptive rather than actionable. A practitioner reading this paper learns \"some items changed\" but cannot decide what to do about it. The practitioner's guide (Section 4.7) offers generic recommendations, but they follow directly from standard psychometric practice and do not require the specific analysis in this paper.\n\n4. **IRT misspecification undermines the theoretical foundation (Major).** CFI = 0.41 and 98% of models showing |Lz| > 2 indicates catastrophic IRT model misfit. MH-DIF conditions on total score as a proxy for ability, but if the latent structure is fundamentally multidimensional (57 MMLU subjects), total score is a poor proxy. The paper acknowledges this but does not adequately address whether the 31% DIF rate is inflated by the fact that total score inadequately captures the true ability differences between cohorts. The ability-quintile control (2.9% C%) is reassuring but does not rule out more subtle forms of ability confounding in the temporal comparison. The IRT misspecification is not a peripheral concern — it strikes at the core assumption of the MH procedure.\n\n5. **Semi-synthetic injection has low ecological validity (Major).** The injection mechanism (flipping incorrect responses to correct at rate p) simulates an idealized contamination scenario that does not resemble how real contamination manifests. Real contamination produces parametric recall, distractor biases, clustered (non-random) item effects, and continuous partial knowledge — none of which the binary flip model captures. The paper self-acknowledges this in Table 5 (\"HIGH mismatch\" on signal type and response pattern), which raises the question: if the injection mechanism is acknowledged to be unrealistic, what exactly does the ΔTPR number validate? The framework demonstrates internal consistency (MH detects the signal it was designed to detect under conditions favorable to detection), but the transfer to naturalistic contamination is assumed, not demonstrated.\n\n### Minor\n\n6. **GSM1K correlation may be confounded by difficulty (Minor).** DIF-C items are systematically harder (mean difficulty 0.38 vs 0.61, p < 0.001). The correlation between contamination gap and DIF ranking advantage (r = -0.478) could be driven entirely by item difficulty as a confound: harder items naturally show smaller accuracy differences between GSM8K and GSM1K regardless of contamination mechanism. The paper reports a partial correlation controlling for accuracy (r = -0.385, p = 0.027) but not controlling for item difficulty. At N = 33, controlling for one additional variable could plausibly eliminate the effect entirely. The paper should report the partial correlation controlling for difficulty.\n\n7. **Broken bibliography (Minor).** All citations render as \"?\" in the compiled paper. While this may be a compilation issue, it makes the paper unreadable in its current form and prevents verifying that citations are correctly placed.\n\n8. **Missing comparison with concurrent work (Minor).** Growing Pains (Habba et al. 2026) is discussed in related work but not empirically compared. Since that paper also addresses temporal comparability through fixed-parameter calibration, a direct empirical comparison (e.g., do their anchor items overlap with the non-DIF items identified here?) would substantially strengthen the positioning.\n\n9. **Audience and venue fit (Minor).** The paper's primary contributions are psychometric characterization of an existing benchmark and a validation methodology critique. The method is applied, not developed. The most natural audience is a measurement or evaluation workshop (e.g., the ICLR Evaluation Workshop, NeurIPS Datasets and Benchmarks), where the empirical findings would be directly actionable and the psychometric framing would be appreciated without requiring algorithmic novelty. At a top ML venue, the bar for \"what should the community do differently as a result of this paper?\" is higher, and the answer here is unclear beyond \"monitor DIF as a diagnostic,\" which is generic psychometric advice.\n\n10. **Circular reasoning concern in downstream impact (Minor).** DIF flags items that differ across cohorts. Removing them and observing that rankings become more stable across cohorts is tautological — by construction, removing the items that differ most will produce more similar rankings. The circularity ablation (z = -9.5 for DIF-based removal being least disruptive among non-random strategies) addresses this partially, but the fundamental circularity remains: DIF items concentrate discriminative information because they are defined as the items that discriminate between cohorts.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. What is the partial correlation between GSM1K contamination gap and DIF ranking advantage after controlling for item difficulty (not just overall accuracy)?\n\n2. The MH-SIBTEST Jaccard is only 0.48. Can you provide a principled recommendation for practitioners about which method to use, or how to interpret the disagreement?\n\n3. If IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU (CFI = 0.41, 98% misfit), what is the theoretical justification for treating total-score-conditioned MH-DIF results as reflecting genuine differential item functioning rather than artifacts of inadequate ability matching?\n\n4. The injection mechanism flips incorrect→correct. Have you considered or tested more realistic injection mechanisms (e.g., boosting confidence on the correct option, introducing distractor biases, or cluster-correlated injection)?\n\n5. You report that 92.4% of instability is item-specific (unrelated to domain accuracy gains). What are the leading alternative explanations for this item-specific instability, and can any of them be tested with the available data?\n\n6. The critical replacement threshold (~23%) is below the DIF flag rate (31%). How should a practitioner interpret the finding that removing all flagged items would exceed the threshold at which benchmark validity begins to degrade?\n\n7. Given that the ensemble (DIF + ConStat + DVD) does not outperform the best single signal (ConStat), what is the argument for using DIF over ConStat for practical contamination detection, given ConStat's substantially lower FPR (0.067 vs 0.314)?\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n- **Soundness**: 2/4. The IRT misspecification is not adequately addressed. The semi-synthetic injection lacks ecological validity. The 31% number is method-dependent with inadequate discussion of what this means.\n- **Presentation**: 3/4. Well-organized with clear claims. The negative results are commendably transparent. But the broken bibliography and the burying of SIBTEST disagreement are problems.\n- **Contribution**: 2/4. The ground truth meta-critique and cross-subject matching are genuine contributions. But applying an unmodified 1988 method to a new dataset, combined with findings that are descriptive but not actionable, puts this below the contribution threshold for a top venue.\n- **Overall**: 4/10 (Marginally below acceptance threshold)\n- **Confidence**: 4/5. I am familiar with DIF methodology, IRT, and the LLM contamination detection literature. My main uncertainty is whether the community would value the empirical characterization more than I do.\n\n---\n\n## Justification\n\nThis paper is a competent, large-scale application of classical psychometric methods to LLM benchmark evaluation. The ground truth meta-critique (web-overlap labels cannot validate differential detectors) and the cross-subject matching protocol are genuine contributions that should influence future work. The transparency about negative results is exemplary. However, the paper does not clear the novelty bar for ICLR: the method is unmodified from 1988, the headline finding (31% instability) is method-dependent and not actionable, the IRT misspecification undermines the theoretical foundation without adequate remediation, and the semi-synthetic validation demonstrates internal consistency rather than real-world detection capability. The finding that instability does not affect rankings (ρ ≈ 0.997) is reassuring but also diminishes the practical urgency of the work. The paper would be a strong contribution to a psychometrics venue or an evaluation workshop, where the empirical findings would be directly valued and the lack of algorithmic novelty would not be penalized. At ICLR, the question \"what should the ML community do differently as a result of this paper?\" does not have a sufficiently compelling answer.","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260521T042647","overall_score":"4 - Marginally Below Acceptance Threshold"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20615,"cache_read_input_tokens":64962,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":20615,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JmYSHry4Z4A85r6Rtdg3Cs","session_id":"b56c3912-c718-44fa-8fad-b4f0c1958f5c","uuid":"f4fca018-e0a2-4acd-9e2f-6d788ff78c83"}
